==============================================================================
S1 — THE UNCONDITIONAL PAD THEOREM (no English assumption anywhere)
==============================================================================
  Theorem. k i.i.d. uniform on Z_29, independent of p  =>  Dk uniform
  =>  Dc = Dp + Dk uniform for ANY p  =>  P(c_i = c_{i-1}) = 1/29 exactly.
  The plaintext CANNOT affect this. English, Klingon, coordinates, base-29
  digits, a key block, random filler -- all give 3.448%.

  observed doublets           : 86 / 12955  = 0.6638%
  uniform-pad prediction      : 446.7 = 3.4483%   (sd 20.8)
  z                           : -17.37
  P(Binom(n=12955, 1/29) <= 86) = exp(-227.1) = 10^-98.6

  => AN UNFILTERED ONE-TIME PAD IS REFUTED UNCONDITIONALLY. This is a theorem,
     not an inference, and it needs no assumption about the plaintext at all.

  ESCAPE HATCH: could the author have SELECTED a lucky pad instead of
  constructing a filter?  Expected number of pads that must be drawn:
    1 / P = 10^98.6 pads.
    At 10^9 pads/second since the Big Bang (4.4e17 s) one could draw 10^26.
    Selection is infeasible by ~73 orders of magnitude.
  => the doublet suppression was CONSTRUCTED at encryption time by a rule that
     looked at the output. The key is therefore NOT independent of the message.
     'External pad' (a document applied blindly) is excluded; what remains is an
     ALGORITHM with a feedback rule. That is a mechanism, not a pad.

==============================================================================
S2 — POSITIVE CONSTRAINT ON THE PLAINTEXT
==============================================================================
  For ANY key independent of the plaintext (pad, PRNG, hash, raw or TRANSFORMED
  keytext, any long derived key):   P(dbl) >= min_d P_Dp(d).
  So a plaintext-independent key can only reach 0.664% if the PLAINTEXT's
  first-difference distribution already has a hole at or below 0.664%.
  Which plaintexts have such a hole?

  plaintext class                        n   min_d P_Dp(d)  argmin    can reach 0.664% w/ indep key?
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  English prose (KJV)               200000         1.5025%       4                                NO
  author's own LP plaintext            180         1.1173%      17                                NO
  uniform random over 29            200000         3.3820%       0                                NO
  restricted alphabet 20            200000         2.6365%      17                                NO
  restricted alphabet 16 (hex)      200000         1.1265%      14                                NO
  digit string 0-9 (coords)         200000         0.0000%      10                               YES
  prior-stage ciphertext            200000         0.6865%       0                                NO
  REAL LP2 ITSELF                    12956         0.6638%       0                               YES

  READING: every natural-language and every full-alphabet plaintext is EXCLUDED
  from the plaintext-independent-key class. Only RESTRICTED-ALPHABET plaintexts
  (digit strings, coordinates, hex, base-N payloads) leave the door open -- and
  they leave it open trivially, because the unused runes give exact zeros.
  This is a falsifiable, positive prediction, not an absence of evidence:
     IF the key is plaintext-independent, THEN the plaintext uses a restricted
     alphabet -- which is exactly what every English-scoring attack in this repo
     is constructed to be blind to. -> hand-off to lane B6.

==============================================================================
S3 — IS THE 86-DOUBLET RESIDUE A STOCHASTIC THINNING? (the filter's own signature)
==============================================================================
  Repo's pinned model: soft rejection sampling, i.i.d. p_keep~0.18. Then the
  surviving doublets are a Bernoulli(p) thinning of a Bernoulli(1/29) process:
  gaps geometric, index of dispersion (Fano factor) ~= 1 on any binning.
  A rule-based or human pass would leave a NON-stochastic residue.

  86 doublets, 85 gaps. mean gap 150.8 (geometric prediction 150.6), sd 142.4
  geometric sd prediction = sqrt(1-p)/p with p=0.00664 -> 150.1

    bins   Fano (real)   null mean   null sd       z
  ----------------------------------------------------
      10        1.3767      0.8957    0.4186   +1.15
      26        1.0411      0.9592    0.2641   +0.31
      50        0.8381      0.9754    0.1920   -0.72
     100        0.9540      0.9839    0.1409   -0.21

  KS(gaps vs Geometric(p=0.00664)) = 0.0767   critical(0.05) = 0.1475  -> consistent with geometric

  READING: a stochastic residue is consistent with the repo's soft-filter model
  AND with any ciphertext-feedback rule that fires probabilistically. It does NOT
  discriminate pad-plus-filter from feedback-cipher. The filter leaves no
  recoverable fingerprint -- which is itself the reason the two cannot be told
  apart from the ciphertext.

==============================================================================
S4 — WHAT IS ACTUALLY FALSIFIABLE, AND HOW MUCH OF IT HAS BEEN SEARCHED
==============================================================================
  G5 established: no ciphertext-only STATISTIC separates a true pad from a
  long key derived from a short seed. That is PRG indistinguishability, and it
  is a theorem, so no future statistic will do it either.
  The only discriminator is ENUMERATION of the seed space. That makes the
  derived-key hypothesis falsifiable and the true-pad hypothesis NOT falsifiable.
  Asymmetry: you can only ever kill derived-key families one at a time; you can
  never confirm 'true pad'. 'Unfalsifiable' and 'proven' are opposite conditions.

    [x] integer-seeded library PRNGs (glibc/MSVC/MT19937/CPython/Java), unix-second seeds 2011-2015
        2.52e9 decodes — DONE (Round 8 SEED)
    [x] lore/string/date seeds over the same PRNGs
        15,408 decodes — DONE (Round 8 SEED)
    [ ] hash counter/chain-mode keystreams (MD5/SHA-1/SHA-256/SHA-512), HMAC-DRBG
        NOT RUN (queued twice in campaign14/REDTEAM-PROPOSALS.md)
    [ ] stream ciphers (RC4/ARC4, AES-CTR)
        NOT RUN (Round 8's own residue paragraph names RC4 as untested)
    [ ] pp49-51 payload EXPANDED as a PRF seed (RC4/AES-CTR/SHA-CTR/HMAC-DRBG)
        NOT RUN (all payload lanes used it DIRECTLY as key material)

  The repo's own strongest claim ('the pad has no structure') is supported by an
  enumeration of exactly ONE generator family. The families a 2014 author with a
  laptop would most plausibly reach for -- openssl rand, RC4, a SHA-256 counter --
  are the ones not enumerated.

